Repair the complete CommonJS source below and return a corrected complete source file. It must export exactly `{ prismVolume }`. The function accepts three finite, non-negative numbers and returns their product. Non-number, `NaN`, or infinite arguments throw `TypeError`; negative arguments throw `RangeError`. Zero is valid, arguments are not mutated, and no state is retained between calls.

```javascript
function prismVolume(length, width, height) {
  const values = [length, width, height];
  if (values.some((value) => typeof value !== 'number' || !Number.isFinite(value))) {
    throw new TypeError('Dimensions must be finite numbers');
  }
  if (values.some((value) => value < 0)) {
    throw new RangeError('Dimensions cannot be negative');
  }
  return length * width + height;
}

module.exports = { prismVolume };
```

